nodecode-carbonvoice is a rewrite of PhononX's PhononX/hermes-plugin-carbonvoice:

  https://github.com/PhononX/hermes-plugin-carbonvoice
  commit 55867dba9c6645ce493fd955970302e59a4548ea
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 PhononX (LICENSE)

Rewritten: what the plugin's adapter did with Carbon Voice's REST API, as four
tools and one command. The personal access token and the API base URL are the
section's, read from the variable it names. The five calls the adapter made
are the five this cell makes — GET /whoami for the agent's own id and the
account that created it, POST /v3/messages/recent with use_last_updated so a
voice memo that gains its transcript a few seconds after its audio is still
seen, GET /v5/messages/{id} for one message, POST /v5/messages/text with an
idempotency key and a reply_to_message_id so a reply lands in the thread it
answers, and GET /channel/{id} for a conversation's kind and its roster,
which is where a user id becomes a display name. The three transcript shapes
the plugin had to read (v5's flat transcript, v3's text_models with
timecodes, and the flat webhook fields) are read here in the same order, and
an empty one is answered as "still transcribing" exactly as the two-phase
voice delivery made necessary. The self-loop filter is kept as exclude_self,
against the same whoami id; the 8000-character ceiling on a message is the
plugin's own constant; the deny-by-default allowlist, its approval prompts
and its audit log are replaced by the fact that nothing arrives unless the
model asks for it. The Python is Common Lisp here: nlk:http in place of
httpx, one section declaration in place of the plugin's environment-variable
wizard, and nlk:define-contract for each tool's arguments, so the schema the
model is shown and the body that runs are one shape.

Left out: the Hermes gateway platform half — the Socket.IO connection, the
REST polling loop, the cursor file that catches up messages received while
the agent was down, the acknowledgement reaction, mark-as-read, the owner
approval prompts and the rejected-sender audit log, the thread-context cache,
the outbound duplicate-suppression window, inbound attachment downloads and
the voice-memo replies through a TTS pipeline. Those belong to a bot that
runs continuously beside a gateway; here the model reads the inbox when it
needs to and answers from the same account. Also left out: the /cv-allow-user,
/cv-deny-user and /cv-list-allow-users commands, whose allowlist a tool cell
has nothing to gate, and the reaction handlers of the approval flow.
